---
/**
 * /commercial-stucco/ — commercial pillar hub (keyword-plan Pillar 5,
 * architecture :264, sequencing :412 Wk 9-10).
 *
 * Target: `commercial stucco contractor Daytona Beach`, `condo stucco
 * repair contractor Volusia County`, `HOA stucco repair Volusia`,
 * `multi-family stucco contractor Florida`.
 *
 * Intent: PROCUREMENT, not distress. The reader is a board member, a
 * property manager, or an owner's rep. They are not frightened of their
 * wall — they are managing a liability, a budget line, and usually a date.
 * The homeowner voice used everywhere else on this site is wrong here and
 * would actively cost us the job.
 *
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------
 * VERIFICATION — THE THREE FLAGS THAT UNBLOCKED THIS PILLAR.
 * All three answered by the owner 2026-09-01, recorded in site.ts:
 *   - commercialAndCondoWork: true  — commercial/condo work, licensed
 *     and insured for it.
 *   - occupiedBuildingWork: true    — occupied buildings, access
 *     coordinated around residents.
 *   - multiStoryWork: true          — multi-story stucco work.
 *
 * This pillar could not honestly exist without the third. A milestone
 * building is three habitable stories BY STATUTORY DEFINITION, so a page
 * addressing that market from a company that had never confirmed it works
 * above one storey would be soliciting work it could not verify it could
 * perform. That question sat open across five consecutive builds.
 *
 * WHAT THE FLAGS DO NOT AUTHORIZE — the owner was explicit on both:
 *   - INSURANCE LIMITS ARE NOT VERIFIED. "Licensed and insured for
 *     commercial and condo work" is sayable. No dollar figure, aggregate,
 *     per-occurrence amount, additional-insured language, waiver of
 *     subrogation, or certificate detail may appear. A board WILL ask for
 *     limits; that is a conversation, and the page routes it to one.
 *   - ACCESS EQUIPMENT AND METHODS ARE NOT VERIFIED. No lift, boom,
 *     scissor, swing stage, scaffold, rope access, or storey ceiling is
 *     named or implied anywhere. "Multi-story" is the whole of the fact.
 *     An unstated ceiling is honest; a guessed one is not.
 *
 * STILL NULL, STILL OMITTED RATHER THAN STUBBED:
 *   [N COMPLETED MULTI-FAMILY PROJECTS], [SQ FT / UNIT COUNTS],
 *   [PROPERTY MANAGER / BOARD REFERENCES], [SAMPLE DOCUMENTATION PACKAGE],
 *   [ENGINEER / CONSULTANT RELATIONSHIPS], [LICENSE #], [PHONE],
 *   [WARRANTY TERM]. keyword-plan:469-474 lists these as pillar blockers;
 *   they are PROOF, not CAPABILITY, so their absence weakens the page
 *   without making it dishonest. Do not invent a project count to fill the
 *   gap — that is the exact failure site.ts exists to prevent.
 *
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------
 * THE OWNABLE ANSWER. Live SERP 2026-09-01 (`commercial stucco contractor
 * Daytona Beach Volusia County condo HOA property manager`): the ground is
 * CONTESTED, unlike the milestone/SIRS SERP which is contractor-empty.
 * FCC Builders explicitly holds "preferred partner for HOAs and commercial
 * property managers" with 15+ years local. Houzz and BBB directories fill
 * the rest, alongside HOA MANAGEMENT companies (Vesta, Empire, Intracoastal)
 * who are not competitors at all — they are the buyer.
 *
 * So this page does NOT try to out-claim FCC on generic "we serve HOAs"
 * language, which is their established ground and a claim we cannot
 * outweigh with zero published project history. It competes where the
 * whole SERP is silent: EVERY result sells the repair, and NOT ONE explains
 * how an association actually gets from "the walls look bad" to a scope it
 * can bid, fund, and defend to its members. The board's real problem is
 * not finding a contractor — it is not knowing what it is buying, and
 * being unable to compare two quotes that describe different work.
 *
 * That is also the honest position for a company with verified capability
 * and no published proof: compete on clarity about the PROCESS, where our
 * lack of a reference list does not matter, rather than on claims of
 * experience we cannot substantiate.
 *
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------
 * THE FIREWALL. keyword-plan brief: "Never linked from homeowner content."
 * Separate funnel, separate buyer, separate CTA. This page links OUT to
 * /contact/ and (once built) its two children. NO homeowner page links in,
 * and this page does NOT link into the homeowner funnel — no /stucco-repair/,
 * no /problems/*, no /stucco-crack-triage/. A board member routed into
 * "is my crack serious?" content has been handed the wrong site.
 *
 * NO PRICE RENDERS, AND `pricingDisclaimer` IS DELIBERATELY NOT IMPORTED.
 * All nine `repairPriceRanges` entries are single-property homeowner repair
 * jobs. None describes a 40-unit elevation, and publishing homeowner
 * numbers to a board would misrepresent the scale of the work. The
 * disclaimer attaches to published ranges; there are none here. If
 * commercial pricing is ever verified, a disclaimer written for commercial
 * work must ship WITH it.
 *
 * CHILDREN — PILLAR COMPLETE 2026-09-01. Both are built and linked:
 * /commercial-stucco/milestone-inspection-repairs/ (statutory deadline
 * intent) and /commercial-stucco/condo-hoa-stucco-repair/ (funding and
 * responsibility intent). This page targets neither keyword; it hands
 * deadline intent to the first and who-pays intent to the second, and keeps
 * the procurement-process ground for itself.
 * /commercial-stucco/sirs-exterior-wall-reserves/ stays REJECTED (failed the
 * splitting test); SIRS lives on the milestone page.
 *
 * STATUTE IS ATTRIBUTED, NEVER ADVISED. FS 553.899 and FS 718.112(2)(g) are
 * public law, cited as public law. This page is not legal advice and does
 * not tell an association what its obligations are. Milestone inspections
 * focus on STRUCTURAL integrity and explicitly exclude cosmetic stucco
 * cracking (verified 2026-09-01) — so the page must never claim milestone
 * inspections are "about stucco." Phase 2 removes cladding to reach
 * structure, and a licensed PE writes the repair specification. We are the
 * trade that closes the wall back up, downstream of the engineer.
 *
 * NOT A HOME INSPECTION. Florida licenses inspectors separately. We do not
 * inspect, and we are not the engineer. Both boundaries are stated.
 *
 * NO WATERPROOFING AS A STANDALONE OFFER (`standaloneWaterproofing: false`)
 * and NO CONCRETE SPALLING (`concreteSpallingRepair: false`) — both are
 * verified FALSE and both are acute risks on THIS page, because balcony
 * spalling and building-envelope waterproofing are exactly what a condo
 * board thinks it is shopping for. Named and routed out explicitly, so no
 * capability is implied by adjacency.
 *
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------
 * VISUAL SYSTEM (redesigned 2026-09-02): migrated from this page's own local
 * hero/prose CSS onto the shared interior-page components - PageHero,
 * SectionHead, Breadcrumbs (via PageHero), FaqList - and the global utilities
 * in Layout.astro (.section, .slab, .joint, .grid-hairline, .card-link,
 * .on-dark, .prose). COPY, FACTS, SCHEMA, URLS, HEADING TEXT AND INTERNAL
 * LINKS ARE UNCHANGED by that migration; only the markup carrying them moved.
 *
 * The two children now render as .card-link cells rather than inline links
 * buried in prose, which is what /problems/ does for its five. Their hrefs and
 * labels are unchanged.
 *
 * THE CTA IS STILL BESPOKE AND MUST STAY THAT WAY. The shared <ContactCta />
 * is NOT used and is NOT imported. It speaks in the homeowner's singular
 * first person and routes to the paid residential Moisture Check; neither
 * addresses a board. The commercial CTA keeps its own markup and copy and
 * simply sits in the closing .section.on-dark band that the interior pages
 * use for their CTA, so the page ends the way the rest of the site does
 * without adopting the residential voice.
 *
 * Breadcrumbs now come from PageHero, which emits BreadcrumbList JSON-LD
 * itself. This page previously emitted none, so nothing was removed.
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------
 */
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../../components/SiteFooter.astro';
import MobileStickyBar from '../../components/MobileStickyBar.astro';
import PageHero from '../../components/PageHero.astro';
import SectionHead from '../../components/SectionHead.astro';
import FaqList from '../../components/FaqList.astro';
import { business, serviceArea, email } from '../../config/site';

const title =
  'Commercial & Condo Stucco Repair | Daytona Beach & Volusia County';
const description =
  'Stucco and exterior wall repair for condominium associations, HOAs, and commercial property in Volusia County. How an association gets from a problem wall to a scope it can bid, fund, and defend.';

/**
 * The three ways this buyer arrives. Written as situations, not services —
 * a board member does not search for "stucco services", they search for the
 * situation they are in. Each names what it is and what it is NOT, because
 * the boundaries are where an association gets misled.
 */
const arrivals = [
  {
    kind: 'A milestone inspection flagged the exterior',
    body: 'A Phase 1 inspection found something, or a Phase 2 report has landed and names exterior wall work. Under FS 553.899 required repairs must begin within 365 days of the Phase 2 report unless the local building department requires earlier. The engineer writes the repair specification; the association bids it out. We are the trade that performs the stucco and exterior wall scope and closes the wall back up.',
    note: 'Milestone inspections assess structural integrity and explicitly set aside cosmetic issues like hairline cracking. If your report names exterior walls, it is not describing a paint problem.',
    href: '/commercial-stucco/milestone-inspection-repairs/',
    hrefLabel: 'Milestone inspection stucco &amp; exterior wall repairs',
  },
  {
    kind: 'A reserve study needs a real number',
    body: 'A Structural Integrity Reserve Study has to cover exterior painting and waterproofing among its required components under FS 718.112(2)(g). The general completion deadline was December 31, 2025; an association whose milestone inspection is due on or before December 31, 2026 may complete the study alongside it, but no later than that date. Reserve specialists need defensible costs for exterior wall work rather than a placeholder, and that requires someone to actually look at the building.',
    note: 'We can scope and price the exterior wall portion. We are not reserve specialists and do not prepare the study itself.',
  },
  {
    kind: 'The walls are simply failing',
    body: 'No statute, no deadline — cracking across multiple elevations, stains spreading below windows, patches that were repaired twice and came back. This is the most common arrival and the one with the least structure around it, because nothing external is forcing the association to define what it actually wants done.',
    note: 'This is where associations most often buy the wrong scope, because nobody has established what is causing the failure before pricing the repair.',
    href: '/commercial-stucco/condo-hoa-stucco-repair/',
    hrefLabel: 'Condo &amp; HOA stucco repair &mdash; who pays and how it gets funded',
  },
];

/**
 * THE GIVEAWAY, and the page's reason to exist. Every ranking competitor
 * sells the repair. None explains the procurement path. Publishing it costs
 * us nothing we should be winning on price anyway, and it is the one thing
 * a board cannot get from the rest of the SERP.
 */
const path = [
  {
    step: 'Establish what is actually wrong',
    body: 'Before anything is priced, someone has to determine whether the walls are failing cosmetically or whether water is moving through the assembly. These produce completely different scopes at completely different costs, and they look similar from the parking lot. An association that skips this step is not comparing bids — it is collecting guesses.',
  },
  {
    step: 'Get the scope in writing before the number',
    body: 'A defensible scope names the elevations, the approximate area, the preparation, what happens where damage is found behind the finish, and how the finish is matched. A quote that is one line and one price cannot be compared to anything, and it cannot be defended to an owner who asks why the assessment went up.',
  },
  {
    step: 'Make every bidder price the same document',
    body: 'The single most common failure in association procurement: three quotes that appear to be for the same job and are not. One includes repairing what is found behind the wall, one prices only what is visible, one includes paint. The spread looks like a price difference. It is a scope difference, and the low bid becomes the high bid once the wall is open.',
  },
  {
    step: 'Agree what happens when the wall opens',
    body: 'Exterior wall work uncovers conditions that were not visible when it was priced. That is normal and it is not a surprise to anyone who has done this work. What matters is that the contract says in advance how a change is documented, approved, and priced, so a board is not making that decision under pressure with residents watching.',
  },
  {
    step: 'Plan the building around the residents',
    body: 'Occupied buildings need access coordinated around the people living in them. Which elevations when, how residents are notified, what gets moved off balconies, what the working hours are. We work occupied buildings and coordinate access with the association or manager rather than leaving it to be sorted out on the first morning.',
  },
];

const boundaries = [
  {
    label: 'We are not the engineer',
    body: 'We do not perform milestone inspections and do not write Phase 1 or Phase 2 reports. Florida requires those from a licensed engineer or architect, and the repair specification for structural work comes from a PE. We perform the stucco and exterior wall scope.',
  },
  {
    label: 'We do not sell waterproofing on its own',
    body: 'Waterproofing may form part of a wall repair, but we do not offer building-envelope waterproofing as a standalone contract. If that is the scope an association is procuring, it needs a waterproofing contractor.',
  },
  {
    label: 'We do not do concrete spalling repair',
    body: 'Rusting rebar, spalling balcony edges, and failing stair landings are outside our work. This is one of the most common findings on a coastal condominium, and it frequently sits directly beside stucco damage on the same elevation, so it is worth naming plainly rather than discovering at contract stage.',
  },
  {
    label: 'We do not perform home or building inspections',
    body: 'Florida licenses inspectors separately. We assess walls to scope and price the work we would perform, which is a different thing from an inspection and should not be substituted for one.',
  },
];

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Do you work with condominium associations and HOAs?',
    a: 'Yes. We perform commercial and condominium stucco work and are licensed and insured for it. Associations, property managers, and owners’ representatives are a distinct part of what we do, and the work is run differently from a single-family repair — the scope goes in writing first, the schedule is coordinated with the manager, and access is planned around residents rather than around us.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can you work on a multi-story building?',
    a: 'Yes, we perform multi-story stucco work. What the access looks like on a specific building depends on the building — its height, the elevations involved, what surrounds it at ground level, and what the association will allow on site. That gets established when we look at the property, not before.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What are your insurance limits?',
    a: 'We are licensed and insured for commercial and condominium work. Specific limits, certificate holders, and any additional-insured or endorsement requirements are handled directly with the association or its manager, because the requirements differ from building to building and most associations have their own. Ask us and we will provide current documentation.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can you work while the building stays occupied?',
    a: 'Yes. Nearly all condominium and commercial stucco work happens in an occupied building, and we coordinate access around residents with the association or property manager — which elevations are worked when, how residents are notified, and what needs to be clear before work starts on a given face of the building.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Our milestone report named the exterior walls. What happens next?',
    a: 'A Phase 2 report identifying substantial structural deterioration leads to a repair program, and under FS 553.899 required repairs must begin within 365 days of that report unless the local building department requires earlier. The repair specification is written by a licensed engineer, and the association bids that specification out. Our part is the stucco and exterior wall scope. Bring us the report and we will tell you what the wall work in it involves.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why do our three quotes vary so much?',
    a: 'Usually because they are not for the same work. Exterior wall quotes diverge on what happens when damage is found behind the finish, whether the price covers all elevations or only the worst one, whether preparation and finish matching are included, and whether paint is in or out. Until every bidder is pricing one written scope, the spread between quotes is describing scope, not value — and the lowest number generally belongs to the narrowest scope.',
  },
];

const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: f.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
  })),
};

/**
 * Service schema. `areaServed` renders because `serviceArea` is now VERIFIED
 * (2026-09-01, county-level). No `offers`, no `priceRange` — nothing about
 * commercial pricing is verified.
 */
const serviceSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Service',
  name: 'Commercial and condominium stucco repair',
  serviceType: 'Commercial stucco and exterior wall repair',
  provider: { '@type': 'LocalBusiness', name: business.name },
  ...(serviceArea ? { areaServed: serviceArea.map((a) => ({ '@type': 'Place', name: a })) } : {}),
  description:
    'Stucco and exterior wall repair for condominium associations, HOAs, and commercial property in Volusia and Orange County, Florida.',
};
---

<Layout title={title} description={description}>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(serviceSchema)} />
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} />

  <SiteHeader current="/commercial-stucco/" />

  <main id="main">
    {/*
      HERO. Single column, no aside - the aside slot is where interior money
      pages put <ContactCta variant="inline" />, which is the homeowner
      component and is deliberately not used anywhere in this cluster.
      Eyebrow, h1 and lede carry the same strings the old local hero rendered.
      Actions route to the two children, which is where a board with a live
      situation should go first.
    */}
    <PageHero
      crumbs={[{ href: '/commercial-stucco/', label: 'Commercial & condo stucco' }]}
      eyebrow="Commercial & condo · Volusia County"
      title="Commercial & condo stucco repair"
      lede="Exterior wall work for condominium associations, HOAs, and commercial property across Volusia County. Most of what an association needs is not a contractor — it is a scope clear enough to bid, fund, and explain to the people paying for it. That comes first here."
      actions={[
        { href: '/commercial-stucco/milestone-inspection-repairs/', label: 'Milestone inspection repairs' },
        { href: '/commercial-stucco/condo-hoa-stucco-repair/', label: 'Condo & HOA repair', variant: 'secondary' },
      ]}
    />

    <section class="section">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            We perform commercial and condominium stucco work and are licensed and
            insured for it, including multi-story buildings and buildings that stay
            occupied while the work runs. What follows is how this work actually
            gets procured, because that is the part nobody publishes and the part
            where associations lose the most money.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    {/* THE THREE ARRIVALS — situations, not services. */}
    <section class="section slab joint">
      <div class="wrap">
        <SectionHead
          eyebrow="Three arrivals"
          title="Where associations usually start"
        />

        <div class="arrivals">
          {arrivals.map((a) => (
            <section class="block">
              <h3>{a.kind}</h3>
              <p>{a.body}</p>
              <p class="note">{a.note}</p>
              {a.href && (
                <p class="more">
                  <a href={a.href} set:html={a.hrefLabel} />
                </p>
              )}
            </section>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    {/* THE GIVEAWAY — the page's reason to exist. */}
    <section class="section">
      <div class="wrap">
        <SectionHead
          eyebrow="The procurement path"
          title="Getting from a problem wall to a scope you can bid"
          lede="These five steps are the difference between an association that buys the repair it needed and one that pays twice. None of them require us."
        />

        <ol class="path prose">
          {path.map((p) => (
            <li>
              <h3>{p.step}</h3>
              <p>{p.body}</p>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ol>
      </div>
    </section>

    {/*
      THE TWO CHILDREN as .card-link cells. Same hrefs and same labels the
      prose sections above already carry; this is a second, scannable route to
      them, which is what a hub is for. Descriptions are compressed from each
      child's own lede - nothing asserted here that those pages do not argue.
    */}
    <section class="section slab joint">
      <div class="wrap">
        <SectionHead
          eyebrow="Go deeper"
          title="If your building is already in one of these situations"
        />

        <div class="grid-hairline cols-2">
          <a class="card-link" href="/commercial-stucco/milestone-inspection-repairs/">
            <h3>Milestone inspection &amp; exterior wall repairs</h3>
            <p>
              Your inspection names exterior wall work, or it is still ahead of
              you. What the statute requires, the open wall nobody quotes for, and
              how to turn an engineer&rsquo;s report into a scope you can bid.</p>
            <span class="card-go">Read the page &rarr;</span>
          </a>

          <a class="card-link" href="/commercial-stucco/condo-hoa-stucco-repair/">
            <h3>Condo &amp; HOA stucco repair</h3>
            <p>
              Who owes for it, what a special assessment notice has to contain,
              and why balconies and parapets do not necessarily share a payer or
              a trade with the wall between them.
            </p>
            <span class="card-go">Read the page &rarr;</span>
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="wrap">
        <SectionHead
          eyebrow="Boundaries"
          title="What we do not do"
          lede="Worth establishing early, because several of these sit directly beside stucco damage on the same building and an association should not find out at contract stage."
        />

        <div class="grid-hairline cols-2">
          {boundaries.map((b) => (
            <div class="entry">
              <h3>{b.label}</h3>
              <p>{b.body}</p>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    {/*
      COST. No number renders and `pricingDisclaimer` is not imported - see the
      doc block. This section exists to say why there is no number, which a
      board reads as candour rather than evasion.
    */}
    <section class="section slab joint">
      <div class="wrap">
        <SectionHead eyebrow="Cost" title="What this costs" />

        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            We publish repair price ranges for single-property work elsewhere on
            this site. We are deliberately not repeating them here, because they
            describe a house and would misrepresent the scale of an association
            job &mdash; a multi-elevation building is not a large version of a
            residential repair, and anyone quoting one from the other is guessing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Commercial and condominium work is priced against a written scope after
            the building has been looked at. What moves the number is the number of
            elevations involved, how much of the damage turns out to be surface
            versus water in the assembly, access, finish matching, and how the work
            has to be staged around residents.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="faq" aria-labelledby="faq-head">
      <div class="wrap">
        <SectionHead
          id="faq-head"
          eyebrow="FAQ"
          title="Common questions from boards and managers"
        />
        <FaqList items={faqs} />
      </div>
    </section>

    {/*
      COMMERCIAL CTA — deliberately NOT the shared <ContactCta />, which is not
      imported on this page. That component is written in the homeowner's voice
      ("send me a photo of the wall and I'll tell you straight"), speaks as a
      single person, and routes to the paid Moisture Check, which is a
      residential diagnostic product. None of that addresses a board.
      The markup and copy below are unchanged from the pre-redesign CTA; only
      the band around it is new, so this page ends in the same dark closing
      band the rest of the site uses without borrowing the residential voice.
      PHONE stays null-gated sitewide; no call affordance renders here either,
      and no number is hardcoded.
    */}
    <section class="section on-dark">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="cta-commercial">
          <h2>Send us the building</h2>
          <p>
            If you have a Phase 2 report, an engineer&rsquo;s specification, a
            reserve study, or just an elevation that keeps getting worse, send it
            over and we will tell you what the exterior wall scope in it involves
            and what we would need to see on site to price it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Association and property-manager enquiries are handled directly
            rather than through the residential form.
          </p>
          {email && (
            <p class="cta-line">
              <a href={`mailto:${email}`}>{email}</a>
            </p>
          )}
          <p class="cta-line">
            <a href="/contact/">Contact page</a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <SiteFooter />

  <MobileStickyBar />
</Layout>

<style>
  /*
   * PAGE-SPECIFIC ONLY. The hero, bands, section heads, card cells, grid,
   * breadcrumbs and FAQ come from the shared components and the global
   * utilities in Layout.astro; those rules were deleted here rather than
   * duplicated. What remains is this page's own furniture.
   */

  .arrivals .block + .block { margin-top: 2.5rem; }

  .arrivals h3,
  .entry h3,
  .path h3 { margin: 0 0 0.5rem; font-size: 1.125rem; }

  .arrivals p,
  .entry p { line-height: 1.7; margin: 0 0 1rem; color: var(--ink-soft); }

  .entry p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }

  /* The qualifying line under each arrival — a boundary, not a sales point. */
  .note {
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    color: var(--ink-faint);
    border-left: 2px solid var(--rule);
    padding-left: 0.875rem;
    margin-bottom: 0 !important;
  }

  .more { margin-top: 0.75rem; margin-bottom: 0 !important; font-weight: 600; }

  .path { max-width: 46rem; }
  .path li { margin-bottom: 2rem; }
  .path li:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }

  /*
   * The commercial CTA sits on the dark closing band. It keeps the raised
   * inner panel the interior pages use, restyled onto --shell tokens by
   * .on-dark rather than the light --paper-warm it used before.
   */
  .cta-commercial {
    max-width: 46rem;
    padding: 2rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--shell-rule);
    border-radius: 2px;
    background: #1d1f22;
  }

  .cta-commercial h2 { margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 1rem; font-size: 1.5rem; }

  .cta-commercial p { line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 1rem; }

  .cta-line { margin-bottom: 0.375rem; font-weight: 600; }
  .cta-line:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
</style>
